---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import ImageFigure from '../components/ImageFigure.astro';
import AdaptivePicture from '../components/AdaptivePicture.astro';
import OrbitalDiagram from '../components/OrbitalDiagram.astro';
---

<BaseLayout>
  <Hero />

  <main>
    <section class="introduction page-gutter" id="world" aria-labelledby="world-title">
      <div class="introduction__meta">
        <p>Maia System</p>
        <p>Class-M · 0.8 g</p>
        <p>Slightly larger than Earth</p>
        <p>Primary settlement · Maia Station</p>
      </div>
      <div class="introduction__copy">
        <p class="kicker">The world in one breath</p>
        <h2 id="world-title">A world beyond survival.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Maia IV orbits within the stable Maia trinary system—a low-gravity world, slightly larger than Earth, with tangerine skies, pale celadon clouds, native photosynthetic aeroplankton, and fungal forests built to an extraordinary scale.</p>
        <p>Nearly the entire permanent population lives at Maia Station, whose civic and medical districts hold 1.0 g. Beyond its cultivated perimeter, much of the planet remains protected for ecological study, archaeology, and xenoanthropology.</p>
        <nav class="archive-paths" aria-label="Related planetary records">
          <a class="archive-link" href="#planetary-profile">Planetary profile ↘</a>
          <a class="archive-link" href="#human-presence">Population register ↘</a>
        </nav>
        <p class="world-maxim">The Queens are felt through light.<br />The Fates are seen in darkness.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="heritage page-gutter" aria-labelledby="heritage-title">
      <div class="heritage__heading">
        <p class="kicker">The world before arrival</p>
        <h2 id="heritage-title">The pattern was already here.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="heritage__body">
        <p class="heritage__lede">Long before Humanity traveled between stars, Maia IV was home to an advanced civilization organized around a base-3 understanding of mathematics, time, ecology, and meaning.</p>
        <div class="heritage__columns">
          <p>The civilization vanished in deep antiquity. Its inscriptions, durable materials, foundations, and protected sites survived in enough detail to reveal a culture shaped by three stars, three moons, and recurring triadic systems.</p>
          <p>The Fates are not Federation inventions. Their associations with beginning, measure, and ending descend from the world’s surviving record. Their familiar names are Federation Standard renderings of far older ideas.</p>
        </div>
        <aside class="heritage__record" aria-label="Planetary protection record">
          <span>Protection authority</span>
          <strong>Federation Bureau of Planetary Heritage</strong>
          <div>
            <p>Ecological preserves, archaeological landscapes, and intact cultural sites beyond Maia Station are held under protected status.</p>
            <nav class="archive-paths archive-paths--compact" aria-label="Related heritage records">
              <a class="archive-link" href="#cultural-record">Cultural record ↘</a>
              <a class="archive-link" href="#protected-status">Protected status ↘</a>
            </nav>
          </div>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="city-story page-gutter" aria-labelledby="station-title">
      <ImageFigure
        name="qt-life-001"
        alt="Three Sisters Roasters and the public promenade at Maia Station"
        label="Maia Station · Civic promenade"
        caption="Daily life unfolds beneath the Fates, where transit, research, neighborhood life, and a morning at Three Sisters Roasters overlap."
        sizes="(min-width: 900px) 58vw, 100vw"
      />
      <div class="city-story__copy">
        <p class="kicker">Ordinary life</p>
        <h2 id="station-title">Maia Station is not a retreat from the world.</h2>
        <p>It is the planet’s only major population center: residents, clinicians, schools, research teams, cafés, visitors, and people who arrived for six weeks and stayed twelve years.</p>
        <nav class="archive-paths" aria-label="Related Maia Station record">
          <a class="archive-link" href="#human-presence">Maia Station record ↘</a>
        </nav>
        <dl class="facts">
          <div><dt>Station gravity</dt><dd>1.0 g</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Native gravity</dt><dd>0.8 g</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Known for</dt><dd>Adaptive medicine</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="landscape page-gutter" aria-labelledby="landscape-title">
      <div class="section-heading section-heading--split">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Beyond the station</p>
          <h2 id="landscape-title">The cultivated edge.</h2>
        </div>
        <p>Maglev lines leave Maia Station for agricultural belts, research preserves, and the planet’s native-gravity wilderness.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="image-figure adaptive-figure">
        <AdaptivePicture
          variants={[
            { key: 'day', name: 'qt-maglev-day' },
            { key: 'night', name: 'qt-maglev-night' },
          ]}
          alt="The maglev route between Maia Station, cultivated land, and the fungal preserves"
          scheme="day-night"
        />
        <figcaption>
          <span>Lachesis Agricultural Belt</span>
          <p>The outer maglev crosses the boundary between the station’s controlled gravity and the giant fungal preserves beyond.</p>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="night-transit" aria-labelledby="night-title">
      <div class="night-transit__statement page-gutter">
        <p class="kicker">The architecture of self</p>
        <h2 id="night-title">When survival is no longer the work,<br /> becoming yourself is.</h2>
        <div class="pathways">
          <article><span>01</span><h3>Release</h3><p>What no longer needs to be carried.</p></article>
          <article><span>02</span><h3>Reflection</h3><p>What becomes visible when urgency quiets.</p></article>
          <article><span>03</span><h3>Renewal</h3><p>What a person chooses to become next.</p></article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="orbital page-gutter" aria-labelledby="orbital-title">
      <div class="orbital__copy">
        <p class="kicker">Planetary context</p>
        <h2 id="orbital-title">Maia IV</h2>
        <p>The Queens rule the system.<br />The Fates circle the world.</p>
        <p>Three natural satellites preserve the oldest names in the planetary record.</p>
        <nav class="archive-paths" aria-label="Related orbital records">
          <a class="archive-link" href="#stellar-system">Stellar architecture ↘</a>
          <a class="archive-link" href="#natural-history">Satellite record ↘</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
      <ImageFigure
        name="qt-orbit-001"
        alt="Maia IV from high orbit with its tangerine atmosphere and celadon weather systems"
        label="Maia IV · High orbit"
        caption="A 0.8-g Class-M world in the Maia trinary system."
        sizes="(min-width: 900px) 66vw, 100vw"
      />

      <div class="planetary-record">
        <dl class="planetary-record__summary" aria-label="Maia IV summary">
          <div><dt>Federation designation</dt><dd>Maia IV</dd></div>
          <div><dt>System</dt><dd>Maia trinary</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Classification</dt><dd>Class-M heritage world</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Surface gravity</dt><dd>0.8 g</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Orbital regime</dt><dd>Maia A/B circumbinary</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Primary settlement</dt><dd>Maia Station</dd></div>
        </dl>

        <article class="reference-block" id="stellar-system">
          <header class="reference-block__heading">
            <p class="kicker">01 · Stellar system</p>
            <h3>The Queens</h3>
          </header>
          <div class="reference-block__content">
            <p class="reference-lede">Maia A and Maia B form a close central binary. Maia I, II, and III occupy the inner system before the inhabited Maia IV. Maia C is the distant third star; Maia V through Maia XII occupy the much larger outer system beyond the red dwarf.</p>
            <OrbitalDiagram />
            <div class="stellar-census">
              <section>
                <span>Maia A · K-type</span>
                <h4>Orange-dwarf primary</h4>
                <p>The dominant member of the close central binary and the principal source of usable light and heat.</p>
              </section>
              <section>
                <span>Maia B · Dwarf companion</span>
                <h4>Orange-dwarf secondary</h4>
                <p>Gravitationally bound to Maia A in the tight central stellar pair.</p>
              </section>
              <section>
                <span>Maia C · M-type</span>
                <h4>Remote red dwarf</h4>
                <p>The distant red dwarf beyond Maia IV and the system’s third sustained light source.</p>
              </section>
            </div>
            <dl class="reference-metrics">
              <div><dt>Primary</dt><dd>K-type orange dwarf</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Planetary orbits</dt><dd>P-type · Maia A/B</dd></div>
              <div><dt>System structure</dt><dd>Hierarchical trinary</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Known planets</dt><dd>Maia I through Maia XII</dd></div>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="reference-block" id="planetary-profile">
          <header class="reference-block__heading">
            <p class="kicker">02 · Planetary profile</p>
            <h3>A lighter world</h3>
          </header>
          <div class="reference-block__content record-columns">
            <div>
              <dl class="reference-list">
                <div><dt>Planet type</dt><dd>Temperate terrestrial</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Radius</dt><dd>Slightly larger than Earth</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Surface gravity</dt><dd>0.8 g</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Bulk density</dt><dd>Lower than Earth</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Atmosphere</dt><dd>Human-breathable Class-M environment</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Hydrosphere</dt><dd>Extensive surface water</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h4>Atmosphere and sky</h4>
              <p>Native photosynthetic aeroplankton inhabit the upper atmosphere and cloud-forming layers. Illuminated primarily by Maia A, their pigments—and the size and concentration of the biological aerosols around them—alter how daylight scatters through the atmosphere, contributing to the tangerine sky and pale celadon clouds.</p>
              <p>As Maia IV cools after sunset, the aeroplankton descend from the upper cloud-forming layers into denser air and enter a less active state. With fewer pigment-bearing organisms suspended at altitude—and no direct stellar illumination to scatter—the celadon cast fades through twilight, revealing a deep cobalt sky and the three Fates above.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="reference-block" id="natural-history">
          <header class="reference-block__heading">
            <p class="kicker">03 · Natural history</p>
            <h3>The Fates and the living world</h3>
          </header>
          <div class="reference-block__content record-columns">
            <div>
              <h4>Three natural satellites</h4>
              <ul class="fates-list">
                <li><strong>Clotho</strong><span>Creation and emergence</span></li>
                <li><strong>Lachesis</strong><span>Continuity, measure, and choice</span></li>
                <li><strong>Atropos</strong><span>Ending and transformation</span></li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h4>Indigenous biosphere</h4>
              <ul class="editorial-list">
                <li>Photosynthetic aeroplankton</li>
                <li>Giant fungal forests and extensive mycelial networks</li>
                <li>Low-gravity megaflora</li>
                <li>Bioluminescent nocturnal organisms</li>
                <li>Native preserve ecosystems under restricted human access</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="reference-block" id="cultural-record">
          <header class="reference-block__heading">
            <p class="kicker">04 · Cultural record</p>
            <h3>The civilization before Humanity</h3>
          </header>
          <div class="reference-block__content record-columns">
            <div>
              <p>Long before Humanity developed interstellar travel, Maia IV supported an advanced indigenous civilization. It had already been extinct for an immense span of time when Federation surveyors arrived.</p>
              <p>The civilization organized mathematics, measurement, architecture, astronomy, language, and symbolic thought around base three. The Queens, the Fates, and their associated symbolism all originate in this culture.</p>
              <p>Xenoanthropology on Maia IV draws from an unusually durable material record, allowing scholars to study a people separated from Humanity by both origin and deep time.</p>
              <p>The cause of its disappearance remains unknown.</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h4>Surviving evidence</h4>
              <ul class="editorial-list">
                <li>Durable architectural remains and monumental inscriptions</li>
                <li>Ceramic and mineral records</li>
                <li>Subsurface repositories</li>
                <li>Mathematical and astronomical documents</li>
                <li>Surviving landscape alignments</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="reference-block" id="protected-status">
          <header class="reference-block__heading">
            <p class="kicker">05 · Protected status</p>
            <h3>A world held in trust</h3>
          </header>
          <div class="reference-block__content record-columns">
            <div>
              <p class="authority-label">Protection authority</p>
              <h4>Federation Bureau of Planetary Heritage</h4>
              <p>Most of Maia IV is protected. Permanent settlement outside designated zones is prohibited.</p>
              <p>Sixty-three agricultural and archaeological field stations support cultivation, conservation, and xenoanthropology within limited research and transit corridors.</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h4>Protected resources</h4>
              <ul class="editorial-list">
                <li>Archaeological sites and untranslated cultural material</li>
                <li>Undisturbed native ecosystems</li>
                <li>Atmospheric biospheres</li>
                <li>Fungal preserves</li>
                <li>Culturally and ecologically sensitive regions</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="reference-block reference-block--station" id="human-presence">
          <header class="reference-block__heading">
            <p class="kicker">06 · Human presence</p>
            <h3>Maia Station</h3>
          </header>
          <div class="reference-block__content">
            <dl class="reference-metrics population-register" aria-label="Maia IV permanent population">
              <div><dt>Permanent population</dt><dd>243,000</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Maia Station</dt><dd>241,500</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Field stations</dt><dd>1,494 · 63 stations</dd></div>
            </dl>
            <div class="record-columns">
              <div>
                <dl class="reference-list">
                  <div><dt>Habitat gravity</dt><dd>1.0 g</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>Function</dt><dd>Settlement, medical center, research community, and cultural gateway</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>Specialties</dt><dd>Adaptive-gravity medicine, physical rehabilitation, and long-term recovery</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>Society</dt><dd>Federation post-scarcity</dd></div>
                </dl>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="reference-lede">Maia Station helps people adapt between different gravitational environments and serves patients recovering from injury, illness, bodily transition, and extended residence on low- or high-gravity worlds.</p>
                <p>Medical cohorts, visiting specialists, research teams, and other temporary residents typically raise the population present on Maia IV to between 265,000 and 280,000.</p>
                <p>Over time, the station also became associated with reflection, reinvention, and personal growth.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="closing page-gutter">
      <p>Queen’s Triad is not where people go to escape their lives.</p>
      <p>It is where they learn how to inhabit them.</p>
      <a href="#top">Return to the light <span aria-hidden="true">↑</span></a>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer class="site-footer">
    <div class="footer-inner">
      <div class="footer-identity">
        <img src="/images/ufp.png" alt="United Federation of Planets emblem" class="footer-emblem" />
        <p class="footer-agency">United Federation of Planets</p>
        <p class="footer-department">Department of Planetary Affairs</p>
      </div>
      <nav class="footer-links" aria-label="Footer navigation">
        <a href="#world">About the Project</a>
        <a href="#disclaimer">Disclaimer</a>
        <a href="mailto:info@queenstriad.org">Contact</a>
      </nav>
      <p class="footer-copyright" id="disclaimer">© 2026 Matthew McClendon. All rights reserved.</p>
    </div>
  </footer>
</BaseLayout>
